WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.835 --> 00:00:03.645
This is an agitator, a mixer in a tank.

2
00:00:04.407 --> 00:00:06.799
On the bridge sit a motor and a gearbox.

3
00:00:07.506 --> 00:00:09.770
Below them the shaft hangs from a coupling,

4
00:00:09.921 --> 00:00:12.824
with the impeller at the bottom: the mixing blades.

5
00:00:13.863 --> 00:00:17.810
A shaft like this rarely breaks from one blow that is too heavy.

6
00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:20.200
Far more often it is fatigue:

7
00:00:20.328 --> 00:00:25.634
a load that comes back millions of times grows a small crack until the shaft snaps,

8
00:00:25.773 --> 00:00:28.652
while the stress stays well below the yield strength.

9
00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:34.280
The liquid keeps pushing the impeller to one side, so the shaft bends a little.

10
00:00:35.011 --> 00:00:35.893
As it turns,

11
00:00:36.114 --> 00:00:38.750
every point of its surface comes in turn to the

12
00:00:38.831 --> 00:00:41.687
side that stretches and the side that is squeezed.

13
00:00:42.426 --> 00:00:45.816
Follow the dot: tension, compression, and tension again.

14
00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:48.831
One revolution is one load cycle.

15
00:00:49.838 --> 00:00:56.595
A shaft at 1,500 revolutions per minute sees more than 2 million load cycles a day.

16
00:00:57.327 --> 00:01:01.971
An agitator turns slower, but still sees many millions within a few years.

17
00:01:03.285 --> 00:01:08.172
This shaft broke twice in 18 months, each time at the end of the keyway.

18
00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:13.367
That is the slot for the key, the steel block that locks the coupling to the shaft.

19
00:01:14.387 --> 00:01:16.790
That is where the stress concentration sits:

20
00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:19.333
the shape makes the stress pile up locally.

21
00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:22.149
The force has to flow around the slot,

22
00:01:22.358 --> 00:01:25.040
and in the sharp corner it all crowds together.

23
00:01:26.054 --> 00:01:29.410
Take a small round hole in a wide plate that you pull on:

24
00:01:29.596 --> 00:01:33.264
at the edge of the hole the stress is about three times as high.

25
00:01:33.997 --> 00:01:36.029
A sharp corner can be worse still.

26
00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:42.394
That is why cracks start at sharp corners, holes, keyways, threads and the weld toe,

27
00:01:42.545 --> 00:01:47.363
where weld metal meets the plate, and also at scratches and corrosion pits.

28
00:01:48.664 --> 00:01:51.833
In that corner a small crack starts, too small to see.

29
00:01:52.536 --> 00:01:56.565
In each period of running it grows a little further into the shaft.

30
00:01:57.603 --> 00:02:01.527
Each stop or change of load leaves a ring on the fracture surface.

31
00:02:02.232 --> 00:02:07.132
Those are beach marks: curved lines, like the lines a wave leaves on the sand.

32
00:02:08.258 --> 00:02:10.209
They spread out from the start point,

33
00:02:10.325 --> 00:02:13.796
so the fracture surface tells you where the crack began: here,

34
00:02:14.005 --> 00:02:15.445
in the corner of the keyway.

35
00:02:16.435 --> 00:02:17.968
That zone is fairly smooth,

36
00:02:18.131 --> 00:02:22.276
sometimes even shiny: the crack faces rubbed against each other.

37
00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:27.091
Meanwhile the metal that is still whole gets smaller and smaller.

38
00:02:27.818 --> 00:02:32.532
The same load rests on a smaller cross-section, so the stress in it rises.

39
00:02:33.551 --> 00:02:36.976
One day the rest is too small, and it breaks at once.

40
00:02:37.715 --> 00:02:41.244
That part is rough and grainy: the final fracture zone.

41
00:02:42.246 --> 00:02:45.032
If the final zone is small, the stress was low,

42
00:02:45.253 --> 00:02:48.608
and the crack had time to grow almost right through the shaft.

43
00:02:49.381 --> 00:02:52.237
A large final zone points to a high stress.

44
00:02:53.248 --> 00:02:54.885
It need not get that far.

45
00:02:55.652 --> 00:03:01.039
On site, you find cracks with dye penetrant: a red liquid that seeps into the crack,

46
00:03:01.202 --> 00:03:03.141
and shows it on a white coating.

47
00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:06.037
How long does a part last?

48
00:03:06.803 --> 00:03:10.890
The S-N curve plots stress against the number of cycles to failure.

49
00:03:11.617 --> 00:03:14.832
The lower the stress, the more cycles it survives.

50
00:03:15.847 --> 00:03:18.239
Many steels show an endurance limit.

51
00:03:18.953 --> 00:03:21.298
Below roughly half their tensile strength,

52
00:03:21.426 --> 00:03:26.988
polished test samples survive 10 million cycles, and are taken to last indefinitely.

53
00:03:27.655 --> 00:03:32.276
The curve usually levels off somewhere between one and ten million cycles.

54
00:03:33.285 --> 00:03:37.743
Aluminium has no real limit: a design states a life in cycles.

55
00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:42.736
And steels too sometimes fail far beyond 10 million cycles.

56
00:03:43.431 --> 00:03:46.798
Infinite life is a useful approximation, not a law.

57
00:03:47.797 --> 00:03:52.999
A real part sits lower than that smooth sample: a rough surface, a larger size,

58
00:03:53.161 --> 00:03:58.281
corrosion, welds and a constant tensile stress on top all take something off.

59
00:03:58.965 --> 00:04:02.831
So a static calculation proves nothing for a vibrating part.

60
00:04:04.184 --> 00:04:05.600
Back to the agitator.

61
00:04:06.336 --> 00:04:09.366
After the first failure a stronger steel went in,

62
00:04:09.575 --> 00:04:11.653
and it broke again in the same place.

63
00:04:12.456 --> 00:04:15.951
High-strength steels are often more sensitive to notches.

64
00:04:16.962 --> 00:04:22.105
First the notch: the keyway gets a rounded end, and where the shaft steps down,

65
00:04:22.279 --> 00:04:23.626
a generous radius.

66
00:04:24.347 --> 00:04:28.375
A smooth surface and shot peening, which puts the skin under compression,

67
00:04:28.515 --> 00:04:29.223
help further.

68
00:04:30.264 --> 00:04:31.112
Then the bending.

69
00:04:31.851 --> 00:04:35.903
A misaligned coupling bends the shaft extra on every revolution:

70
00:04:36.019 --> 00:04:37.273
check the alignment.

71
00:04:38.034 --> 00:04:42.143
And balance the impeller, because unbalance makes the shaft vibrate.

72
00:04:42.915 --> 00:04:44.773
Less bending, much longer life.

73
00:04:45.805 --> 00:04:48.510
Stronger steel did not help, a radius did.

74
00:04:49.283 --> 00:04:53.974
When a stronger part breaks again, think of fatigue: ask about the cycles,

75
00:04:54.148 --> 00:04:56.377
the vibration and the corner radius.
